I have everything needed. Let me confirm the config resolution once more before writing up.

For bf16 / hdim128 / causal / paged (`PagedKVNonTMA=true`), the tile selector at `tile_size.h:44` returns `{128, 128, true, true}` → **kBlockM=128, kBlockN=128, MmaPV_is_RS=true, IntraWGOverlap=true**; `flash_fwd_launch_template.h:48` forces **kStages=2**. `Transpose_V = Is_FP8 && !V_colmajor = false` (mainloop line 57). So `KV_Same_Iter = Transpose_V || !IntraWGOverlap = false`.

---

# Producer (load warpgroup) schedule — bf16, hdim128, causal, paged (page_size=1, PagedKVNonTMA, Use_TMA_KV=false, kStages=2)

All file paths absolute. Resolved compile-time facts: `Transpose_V=false`, `IntraWGOverlap=true`, `Use_TMA_KV=false`, `kStages=2`, `kBlockN=128`, `NumProducerThreads=128`, `KV_Same_Iter=false`.

## 1. K and V are on SEPARATE pipelines/mbarriers with independent `producer_commit`

`.../hopper/mainloop_fwd_sm90_tma_gmma_ws.hpp:295-297` — with `Use_TMA_KV=false` and `Transpose_V=false`, both K and V resolve to distinct `cutlass::PipelineAsync<2>` instances (`pipeline_k`, `pipeline_v`); `pipeline_vt` is unused here:

```cpp
using MainloopPipelineK  = std::conditional_t<Use_TMA_KV, PipelineTmaAsync, typename cutlass::PipelineAsync<kStages>>;
using MainloopPipelineV  = std::conditional_t<!Transpose_V && Use_TMA_KV, PipelineTmaAsync, typename cutlass::PipelineAsync<kStages>>;
using MainloopPipelineVt = std::conditional_t<Use_TMA_KV, PipelineTmaAsync, typename cutlass::PipelineAsync<kStages>>;
```

`load()` receives them as three separate objects (`.../mainloop_...:620-622`). The `load_K` / `load_V` lambdas each acquire/commit their OWN pipeline. `load_K` (`.../mainloop_...:787-798`):

```cpp
auto load_K = [&] (int const n_block, auto const& smem_pipe_write, auto need_seqlenk_masking_type) {
    pipeline_k.producer_acquire(smem_pipe_write);
    if constexpr (!PagedKVNonTMA) {
        ... // TMA path, not taken
    } else {
        constexpr bool Seqlenk_mask = decltype(need_seqlenk_masking_type)::value;
        paged_kv_manager.template load_K<Seqlenk_mask>(n_block, sK_pi(_, _, smem_pipe_write.index()));
        pipeline_k.producer_commit(smem_pipe_write, cutlass::arch::cpasync_barrier_arrive);
    }
};
```

`load_V` (`.../mainloop_...:800-812`) — `pipeline_v_load` resolves to `pipeline_v` since `!Transpose_V`:

```cpp
auto load_V = [&] (int const n_block, auto const& smem_pipe_write, auto need_seqlenk_masking_type) {
    auto pipeline_v_load = cute::conditional_return<!Transpose_V>(pipeline_v, pipeline_vt);
    pipeline_v_load.producer_acquire(smem_pipe_write);
    if constexpr (!PagedKVNonTMA) {
        ... // TMA path, not taken
    } else {
        constexpr bool Seqlenk_mask = decltype(need_seqlenk_masking_type)::value;
        paged_kv_manager.template load_V<Seqlenk_mask>(n_block, sVcpasync(_, _, smem_pipe_write.index()));
        pipeline_v_load.producer_commit(smem_pipe_write, cutlass::arch::cpasync_barrier_arrive);
    }
};
```

So: two independent `PipelineAsync<2>` barrier sets. Each commit is `cpasync_barrier_arrive` — i.e. the mbarrier arrival is deferred until the issuing thread's cp.async group completes; the producer does not spin-wait. The consumer mirrors this with separate `consumer_wait(pipeline_k,...)` / `consumer_wait(pipeline_v,...)` and separate `consumer_release` calls (`.../mainloop_...:1214-1220`, `1259-1262`).

## 2. Load-level IntraWGOverlap: K leads V by one n_block (V lags by one)

The main load loop (`.../mainloop_...:896-920`):

```cpp
int n_block_prev = n_block;
--n_block;
#pragma unroll (!Transpose_V && Use_TMA_KV ? 2 : 1)   // == 1 here (paged, cp.async)
for (; n_block >= n_block_min; --n_block) {
    PipelineState smem_pipe_write_v = smem_pipe_write; // copy the state, write_v is always 1 step behind
    ++smem_pipe_write;
    if (should_load_KV) {
        if constexpr (PagedKVNonTMA) {
            paged_kv_manager.template load_page_table<false /*Seqlenk_mask*/>(n_block);
        } else {
            paged_kv_manager.load_page_table_TMA(n_block);
        }
        if constexpr (Transpose_V) { load_V(n_block, smem_pipe_write, cute::false_type{}); }   // not taken
        load_K(n_block, smem_pipe_write, cute::false_type{} /*Seqlenk_mask*/);
        if constexpr (!Transpose_V) {
            if constexpr (IntraWGOverlap) {
                load_V(n_block_prev, smem_pipe_write_v, cute::true_type{} /*Seqlenk_mask*/);
            } else {
                load_V(n_block, smem_pipe_write, cute::false_type{});
            }
        }
    }
    n_block_prev = n_block;
    if constexpr (Transpose_V) { copy_Vt_to_V(smem_pipe_write_v); }
}
```

The exact interleave for this config: each iteration loads **`load_K(n_block, smem_pipe_write)`** (current block, new stage) and **`load_V(n_block_prev, smem_pipe_write_v)`** (previous block, previous stage). `smem_pipe_write_v` is the pre-increment copy — one stage behind `smem_pipe_write`. So K for block `i` and V for block `i` land in the same stage index, but V is issued one loop iteration later than K. V trails K by exactly one n_block. Note V here carries `Seqlenk_mask = true_type` because the trailing V is the block that was the seqlen-boundary block in the previous step.

Prologue only loads K (see §3); the trailing V for the very last block is flushed after the loop (`.../mainloop_...:922-924`):

```cpp
scheduler_prefetch();
if constexpr (!Transpose_V && IntraWGOverlap) {
    if (should_load_KV) { load_V(n_block_prev, smem_pipe_write, cute::true_type{} /*Seqlenk_mask*/); }
}
```

## 3. How it avoids starving the consumer at only 2 stages — NO deeper prefetch, NO "both stages upfront"

There is **no extra prefetch depth beyond kStages=2**, and the prologue issues only ONE stage's K (not both stages). Prologue (`.../mainloop_...:831-848`):

```cpp
int n_block = n_block_max - 1;

int warp_idx_in_warpgroup = __shfl_sync(0xffffffff, (threadIdx.x / 32) % 4, 0);
static constexpr bool SingleProducerWarp = NumProducerThreads == cutlass::NumThreadsPerWarp;   // false (128)
bool should_load_KV = !Use_TMA_KV || ((SingleProducerWarp || warp_idx_in_warpgroup == 0) && cute::elect_one_sync());  // == true

if (should_load_KV) {
    if constexpr (PagedKVNonTMA) {
        paged_kv_manager.template load_page_table<true /*Seqlenk_mask*/, true /*First_iter*/>(n_block);
    } else {
        paged_kv_manager.template load_page_table_TMA<true /*First_iter*/>(n_block);
    }
    if constexpr (Transpose_V) { load_V(n_block, smem_pipe_write, cute::true_type{}); }   // not taken
    load_K(n_block, smem_pipe_write, cute::true_type{} /*Seqlenk_mask*/);
}
```

So the prologue issues page-table load + `load_K` for `n_block_max-1` into stage 0 only. V is deliberately NOT loaded in the prologue for the IntraWGOverlap path (the `if constexpr (!Transpose_V && !IntraWGOverlap)` prologue-V block at `.../mainloop_...:893-895` is compiled out). Then Q is loaded and the producer waits on `barrier_O` (`.../mainloop_...:890`) before entering the loop.

How starvation is avoided with only 2 stages:
- **cp.async is fire-and-forget.** `load_K`/`load_V` issue `cp.async` then `producer_commit(..., cpasync_barrier_arrive)` — the producer never blocks on load completion; it only blocks at the next `producer_acquire` once both stages are in flight and unreleased. So the producer runs ahead of the consumer by up to `kStages=2` outstanding buffers, hiding gather latency.
- **All 128 threads gather in parallel** (§4), so a single n_block's K (128 rows × hdim128) is issued as many concurrent `cp.async` transactions, not serialized on one thread.
- **K-ahead-of-V overlap** (§2) means that while the consumer runs the P=Q·Kᵀ GEMM on stage `i`'s K, the producer has already committed the next K and is filling V.

The producer depth is strictly the pipeline depth. `producer_acquire` inside `load_K`/`load_V` (`.../mainloop_...:788, 802`) is the only backpressure; with 2 stages the producer can be at most 2 K-buffers and 2 V-buffers ahead. There is no software-managed extra ring or additional prefetch queue.

## 4. Producer threads issuing cp.async, and page-table resolution

**Thread count: 128 (a full warpgroup), not 32.** `.../mainloop_...:124`:

```cpp
static constexpr int NumProducerThreads = !Transpose_V && Use_TMA_KV && Use_TMA_Q ? cutlass::NumThreadsPerWarp : cutlass::NumThreadsPerWarpGroup;
```

With `Use_TMA_KV=false` the ternary picks `NumThreadsPerWarpGroup = 128`. And `should_load_KV = !Use_TMA_KV || (...)` (`.../mainloop_...:836`) short-circuits to **true for all 128 threads** (contrast the TMA path, where a single elected lane issues). So all 128 producer threads run the paged gather.

**Page-table resolution per key (`.../hopper/paged_kv.h`).** Thread tiling constants for hdim128/bf16 (`paged_kv.h:30-45`): `kGmemElemsPerLoad = 16B/2B = 8`; `kBytePerRow = 128 * 2 = 256`; `kBlockKGmem = 128/2 = 64`; hence **`kGmemThreadsPerRow = 64/8 = 8`** — 8 threads cooperate per key-row (one cache line), and threads on the same row are guaranteed in the same warp (`paged_kv.h:40-42`).

The page-table load is a **separate, decoupled gmem read into registers** issued before the K/V data gather. `load_page_table` (`paged_kv.h:133-153`):

```cpp
template <bool Seqlenk_mask=false, bool First_iter=false>
CUTLASS_DEVICE
void load_page_table(const int n_block) {
    #pragma unroll
    for (int i = 0; i < kPageEntryPerThread; ++i) {
        int const row = i * NumThreads + (thread_idx % kGmemThreadsPerRow) * (NumThreads / kGmemThreadsPerRow) + (thread_idx / kGmemThreadsPerRow);
        int const row_idx = n_block * kBlockN + row;
        int page_idx, page_offset;
        page_idx = page_size_divmod.divmod(page_offset, row_idx + leftpad_k);
        int const page = ((i + 1) * NumThreads <= kBlockN || row < kBlockN) && (!Seqlenk_mask || row_idx < seqlen_k) ? mPageTable[page_idx] : 0;
        tPrPageOffset[i] = {page, page_offset};
    }
    if constexpr (First_iter && !KV_Same_Iter) { compute_V_ptr(); }
}
```

With page_size=1, `page_offset` is always 0 and each `row_idx` maps to its own page entry. For this tile `kPageEntryPerThread = ceil_div(size<1>(TensortKcK)=8, kGmemThreadsPerRow=8) = 1`, so each thread reads exactly one page-table entry (uncoalesced-by-design, `paged_kv.h:136-139`). The expensive int64 base-pointer arithmetic is split across the 8 threads-per-row, then broadcast with `__shfl_sync`. `load_K` (`paged_kv.h:233`):

```cpp
Element const* k_ptr = reinterpret_cast<Element const*>(__shfl_sync(0xffffffff, reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(tPrKPtr(m / kGmemThreadsPerRow)), (m % kGmemThreadsPerRow), kGmemThreadsPerRow));
```

and identically in `load_V` (`paged_kv.h:270`). The per-key pointer is computed by one lane (`compute_K_ptr`/`compute_V_ptr`, `paged_kv.h:187-205`) and `__shfl`-broadcast across the 8-thread row group.

**Is the page-table load decoupled/prefetched ahead of the K/V gather?** Yes, and specifically for V it is computed one iteration ahead because `KV_Same_Iter=false`. The manager's own contract (`paged_kv.h:19-21`):

```cpp
// If KV_Same_Iter=false, then we do load_page_table(0), load_K(0), load_page_table(1), load_K(1), load_V(0),
// load_page_table(2), load_K(2), load_V(1), etc.
// So we need to compute the V pointers for the previous iteration.
```

Concretely: `load_page_table(n_block)` fills `tPrPageOffset` for the current block; `load_K` consumes it immediately for K pointers; and V-pointer computation is staged one step behind. `load_V` computes V pointers for the *next* V load at its tail (`paged_kv.h:280`):

```cpp
if constexpr (!KV_Same_Iter) { compute_V_ptr(); }
```

and `get_indices_for_*_TMA` are unused on this cp.async path. So within each iteration the ordering is: page-table gmem read (into regs) → K pointer compute+broadcast → K cp.async gather (current block) → V pointer already resolved from the prior step → V cp.async gather (previous block). The page table is thus resolved ahead of, and decoupled from, the K/V data gather, but only by the single-iteration lag inherent to `KV_Same_Iter=false` — there is no multi-block page-table prefetch queue.
